<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Image Tools</span>
      <h1>TinyPNG vs Squoosh: Which Image Compressor Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      TinyPNG and Squoosh are the two image compressors most often shortlisted by web developers and designers who need to shrink images for faster websites in 2026. TinyPNG wins on batch processing, CMS integrations, and workflow automation. Squoosh wins on format variety, visual comparison, privacy, and advanced controls. This comparison breaks down compression quality, format support, workflow, batch processing, and pricing so you can pick the right image compressor for your project.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://tinypng.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try TinyPNG</a>
      <a href="https://squoosh.app" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Squoosh</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>TinyPNG</th><th>Squoosh</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Batch compression, CMS workflows, automation</td><td>Visual comparison, format control, privacy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>Free up to 20 images, paid API and apps</td><td>Completely free, open source</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Platforms</td><td>Web, macOS, Windows, WordPress, Shopify</td><td>Web (runs in browser)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing Location</td><td>Cloud (TinyPNG servers)</td><td>Local (browser via WebAssembly)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch Compression</td><td>Yes (20 free, unlimited paid)</td><td>One image at a time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Formats Supported</td><td>PNG, JPEG, WebP</td><td>JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, JPEG XL, WP2, MozJPEG</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Visual Comparison</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, side-by-side slider</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lossless Mode</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CMS Plugins</td><td>WordPress, Shopify, Magento</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API Access</td><td>Yes (paid)</td><td>No (open source, self-host)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>File Size Limit (Free)</td><td>5 MB per image</td><td>No limit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Resize on Compress</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is TinyPNG?</h2>
    <p>TinyPNG is an online image compression service launched in 2012 by Voormedia, a Dutch web development studio. TinyPNG became popular for its ability to dramatically reduce PNG and JPEG file sizes with minimal quality loss, using smart lossy compression techniques that reduce the number of colors in the image. By 2026, TinyPNG is one of the most widely used image compressors on the web, with official plugins for WordPress, Shopify, and Magento, plus a paid API for developers.</p>
    <p>The defining characteristic of TinyPNG is its workflow integration. Beyond the simple web interface where you drag and drop images, TinyPNG offers desktop apps for macOS and Windows, official CMS plugins that compress images automatically on upload, and a developer API that integrates into build pipelines and CI/CD systems. This makes TinyPNG the better choice for teams that want compression to happen automatically as part of their existing workflow, without manual steps.</p>

    <h2>What Is Squoosh?</h2>
    <p>Squoosh is an open-source image compression app launched in 2018 by the Google Chrome team. Squoosh was built to demonstrate the capabilities of WebAssembly, running advanced image codecs entirely in the browser without uploading images to a server. By 2026, Squoosh remains the reference tool for developers who want to compare image formats and codecs side by side, with support for modern formats like AVIF, JPEG XL, and WP2 alongside the standard JPEG, PNG, and WebP.</p>
    <p>The defining characteristic of Squoosh is its visual comparison interface. The app splits the screen with a slider, showing the original image on one side and the compressed version on the other, with file size and quality metrics updated in real time as you adjust compression settings. This makes Squoosh the best tool for understanding exactly how compression affects an image, and for choosing the right format and settings for each image. The trade-off is that Squoosh processes one image at a time and has no batch processing or CMS integrations.</p>

    <h2>Compression Quality and Algorithm</h2>
    <p>Both tools produce excellent compression results, but they use different approaches. TinyPNG uses smart lossy compression, while Squoosh gives you access to multiple codecs with fine-grained control.</p>

    <h3>TinyPNG Smart Lossy Compression</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG's compression reduces the number of colors in PNG images and applies optimized quantization to JPEGs, producing file sizes that are typically 50% to 80% smaller than the originals with quality loss that is virtually invisible at normal viewing sizes. The compression is automatic: you upload an image, and TinyPNG applies its optimized settings. This makes TinyPNG fast and foolproof, but it does not let you tune the compression level or compare formats.</p>

    <h3>Squoosh Multi-Codec Control</h3>
    <p>Squoosh gives you access to multiple codecs including MozJPEG, AVIF, JPEG XL, WebP, WP2, and OptiPNG, each with its own quality slider and advanced settings. You can compare codecs side by side with the visual slider, seeing exactly how each affects the image at different quality levels. For users who want maximum control over compression, Squoosh is more capable than TinyPNG. The trade-off is that Squoosh requires more knowledge to use effectively, and tuning each image takes longer than TinyPNG's automatic approach.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best automatic compression goes to <span class="winner-tag">TinyPNG</span>. Best compression control and codec variety goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squoosh</span>.</p>

    <h2>Format Support and Modern Codecs</h2>
    <p>Format support is where Squoosh has a decisive lead. TinyPNG covers the core formats, while Squoosh supports the latest next-generation codecs.</p>

    <h3>Squoosh Next-Gen Codec Support</h3>
    <p>Squoosh supports JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, JPEG XL, WP2, and MozJPEG, giving you access to the latest codecs that produce smaller files than standard JPEG and PNG. AVIF and JPEG XL in particular can reduce file sizes by 50% or more compared to JPEG at the same visual quality. For developers who want to ship the smallest possible images and support modern browsers, Squoosh is the stronger tool.</p>

    <h3>TinyPNG Core Format Focus</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG focuses on the three most widely used formats: PNG, JPEG, and WebP. This covers the vast majority of web images, and WebP support means you can serve modern format images to browsers that support it. However, TinyPNG does not support AVIF, JPEG XL, or other next-generation codecs. For users who need the absolute smallest file sizes with modern codecs, Squoosh is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best format support and modern codecs goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squoosh</span>.</p>

    <h2>Workflow and Batch Processing</h2>
    <p>Workflow and batch processing is where TinyPNG establishes a decisive lead. Squoosh processes one image at a time, while TinyPNG handles batches and integrates into existing workflows.</p>

    <h3>TinyPNG Batch and CMS Integration</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG's web interface accepts up to 20 images at once for free, and the paid API and desktop apps handle unlimited batch processing. More importantly, TinyPNG offers official plugins for WordPress, Shopify, and Magento that compress images automatically when you upload them to your CMS, with no manual steps. The developer API also integrates into build pipelines, so images are compressed as part of your CI/CD process. For teams that want compression to happen automatically, TinyPNG is the clear winner.</p>

    <h3>Squoosh Single-Image Focus</h3>
    <p>Squoosh processes one image at a time in the browser. There is no batch processing, no CMS plugins, and no API. If you need to compress 50 images, you must process each one manually in the Squoosh interface. This makes Squoosh impractical for high-volume workflows, though it is fine for occasional use or for fine-tuning a single hero image. For users who need batch processing, TinyPNG is the only viable choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best workflow and batch processing goes to <span class="winner-tag">TinyPNG</span>.</p>

    <h2>Privacy and Processing Location</h2>
    <p>Privacy and processing location is where Squoosh has a meaningful advantage. TinyPNG uploads images to its servers, while Squoosh processes them locally in your browser.</p>

    <h3>Squoosh Local Processing</h3>
    <p>Squoosh processes images entirely in your browser using WebAssembly, which means your images never leave your device. This is a significant advantage for users who work with sensitive images, such as medical scans, legal evidence, or unreleased product photos, where uploading to a third-party server is not acceptable. Squoosh's local processing also means it works offline once the app is loaded.</p>

    <h3>TinyPNG Cloud Processing</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG uploads images to its servers for processing, then deletes them after a short period. For most web images, this is fine, but it is a limitation for users with strict privacy or security requirements. TinyPNG's privacy policy is clear about deletion practices, but the fact that images transit through external servers is a consideration for sensitive workflows.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best privacy and local processing goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squoosh</span>.</p>

    <h2>Visual Comparison and User Interface</h2>
    <p>Visual comparison is where Squoosh shines. TinyPNG has a simple drag-and-drop interface, while Squoosh offers a side-by-side slider for comparing compression results.</p>

    <h3>Squoosh Side-by-Side Slider</h3>
    <p>Squoosh's interface splits the screen with a slider, showing the original image on one side and the compressed version on the other. As you adjust compression settings or switch codecs, the comparison updates in real time, with file size and quality metrics displayed prominently. This makes Squoosh the best tool for understanding exactly how compression affects an image, and for choosing the right settings for each image. For designers who want to see the quality impact before committing, Squoosh is invaluable.</p>

    <h3>TinyPNG Simple Drag and Drop</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG's interface is a simple drag-and-drop: you upload images, TinyPNG compresses them, and you download the results. There is no visual comparison or real-time adjustment. This makes TinyPNG faster for batch processing but less informative for users who want to understand or tune the compression. For users who just want quick results, TinyPNG's simplicity is an advantage.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best visual comparison and interface goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squoosh</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is one of the differentiators. Both tools are free for basic use, but TinyPNG charges for high-volume API access, while Squoosh is completely free and open source.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>TinyPNG</th><th>Squoosh</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Web Interface</td><td>Free (20 images, 5 MB each)</td><td>Free, unlimited</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desktop App</td><td>$19 one-time (macOS, Windows)</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API (500 compressions/mo)</td><td>$0.009 per compression</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CMS Plugins</td><td>Free (WordPress, Shopify)</td><td>Not available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Self-Host</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, open source</td></tr>
        <tr><td>File Size Limit</td><td>5 MB (free), 50 MB (paid)</td><td>No limit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch Limit</td><td>20 free, unlimited paid</td><td>One at a time</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Both tools are free for basic use, which makes them both excellent choices for occasional compression. TinyPNG's paid tiers are for users who need high-volume API access or desktop apps, and the pricing is reasonable for the value. Squoosh is completely free and open source, with no paid tiers, because it runs entirely in your browser.</p>
    <p>For most users, the choice is not about price but about workflow: if you need batch processing and CMS integration, TinyPNG's paid tiers are worth it; if you need visual comparison and format control, Squoosh is free and more capable.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best free value goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squoosh</span>. Best paid workflow value goes to <span class="winner-tag">TinyPNG</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>TinyPNG Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Batch compression handles up to 20 images free, unlimited paid</li>
        <li>Official plugins for WordPress, Shopify, and Magento</li>
        <li>Developer API integrates into build pipelines and CI/CD</li>
        <li>Desktop apps for macOS and Windows</li>
        <li>Automatic compression requires no tuning</li>
        <li>Smart lossy compression produces 50-80% smaller files</li>
        <li>Free web interface for up to 20 images under 5 MB</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>TinyPNG Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Uploads images to external servers for processing</li>
        <li>Limited format support (PNG, JPEG, WebP only)</li>
        <li>No AVIF, JPEG XL, or next-gen codec support</li>
        <li>No visual comparison or real-time tuning</li>
        <li>Free tier limited to 20 images and 5 MB each</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Squoosh Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Completely free and open source</li>
        <li>Processes images locally in the browser for privacy</li>
        <li>Supports AVIF, JPEG XL, WebP, WP2, MozJPEG, and more</li>
        <li>Side-by-side visual comparison slider</li>
        <li>Real-time compression tuning with quality metrics</li>
        <li>Lossless compression option available</li>
        <li>No file size limits or image count limits</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Squoosh Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Processes one image at a time, no batch mode</li>
        <li>No CMS plugins or API for workflow integration</li>
        <li>Requires more knowledge to use effectively</li>
        <li>Tuning each image takes longer than automatic compression</li>
        <li>No desktop app, browser only</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>WordPress and Shopify Site Owners</h3>
      <p>If you run a WordPress, Shopify, or Magento site and want automatic image compression on upload, TinyPNG is the better choice. Its official plugins compress images in the background without any manual work, keeping your site fast without adding steps to your workflow. Squoosh has no CMS integrations and would require manual compression before each upload.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Developers Who Need Batch Processing</h3>
      <p>If you need to compress hundreds or thousands of images as part of a build pipeline, TinyPNG is the better choice. Its developer API integrates into CI/CD systems, and its desktop app handles unlimited batch processing. Squoosh processes one image at a time and has no API, making it impractical for high-volume workflows.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Designers Who Want Visual Control</h3>
      <p>If you want to see exactly how compression affects an image and tune the settings for each image, Squoosh is the better choice. Its side-by-side slider and real-time metrics let you compare codecs and quality levels before committing. For hero images, product photos, and other high-impact visuals where quality matters, Squoosh gives you the control you need.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Users with Strict Privacy Requirements</h3>
      <p>If you work with sensitive images that cannot leave your device, such as medical scans, legal evidence, or unreleased product photos, Squoosh is the better choice. Its local processing via WebAssembly means images never leave your browser. TinyPNG uploads images to its servers, which is a limitation for sensitive workflows.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Developers Adopting Next-Gen Formats</h3>
      <p>If you want to ship AVIF, JPEG XL, or other next-generation image formats to reduce file sizes by 50% or more, Squoosh is the better choice. Its support for modern codecs lets you test and compare formats before integrating them into your build. TinyPNG does not support next-gen codecs, limiting you to PNG, JPEG, and WebP.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Compressing images for a WordPress blog</h3>
      <p>A blogger running a WordPress site wants every uploaded image to be compressed automatically. The TinyPNG WordPress plugin compresses PNG, JPEG, and WebP images on upload, reducing file sizes by 50% to 80% without any manual work. The blogger uploads images as usual, and the plugin handles compression in the background, keeping the site fast without adding steps to the publishing workflow.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Fine-tuning a hero image for a landing page</h3>
      <p>A designer wants to compress a hero image for a landing page and wants to see exactly how compression affects quality. Squoosh's side-by-side slider lets the designer compare JPEG, WebP, and AVIF at different quality levels, choosing the format and settings that produce the smallest file with acceptable quality. The designer exports the optimized image and ships it, confident in the quality because they saw the comparison in real time.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Batch compressing 500 product images</h3>
      <p>An ecommerce developer needs to compress 500 product images before a site launch. TinyPNG's API integrates into the build pipeline, compressing all 500 images in a single batch process with consistent settings. Squoosh would require processing each image manually, which would take hours instead of seconds, making TinyPNG the only practical choice for high-volume batch compression.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Compressing sensitive medical images locally</h3>
      <p>A medical professional needs to compress medical scan images for a presentation but cannot upload them to a third-party server due to patient privacy regulations. Squoosh processes the images entirely in the browser, so they never leave the professional's device. TinyPNG would require uploading the sensitive images to external servers, which violates the privacy policy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Testing AVIF adoption for a web app</h3>
      <p>A frontend developer wants to test whether AVIF or JPEG XL produces smaller files for their web app's images. Squoosh lets the developer load an image, compare AVIF, JPEG XL, and WebP side by side with the visual slider, and choose the format that produces the smallest file with acceptable quality. TinyPNG does not support AVIF or JPEG XL, making Squoosh the only choice for next-gen format testing.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for workflow and batch processing: TinyPNG.</strong> TinyPNG's batch compression, CMS plugins, developer API, and desktop apps make it the best image compressor for teams that want compression to happen automatically as part of their existing workflow. For WordPress, Shopify, and high-volume pipelines, TinyPNG is the clear choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for visual control and next-gen formats: Squoosh.</strong> Squoosh's side-by-side visual comparison, multi-codec support, local processing, and free open-source model make it the best tool for designers and developers who want maximum control over compression. For hero images, sensitive images, and next-gen format testing, Squoosh is the stronger choice.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for occasional use: Squoosh.</strong> Squoosh is completely free with no limits, making it the best choice for users who only need to compress a few images occasionally and want a capable tool without paying. TinyPNG's free tier is generous but limited to 20 images under 5 MB.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For organizations with diverse needs, run TinyPNG for automatic CMS compression and batch pipelines, and Squoosh for fine-tuning hero images and testing next-gen formats. This hybrid approach uses each tool where it is strongest and avoids forcing one tool into a use case it was not designed for.</p>
    </div>

