    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is TinyPNG or Squoosh completely free?</h3>
    <p>Both are free for basic use, but with different limits. TinyPNG's web interface is free for up to 20 images under 5 MB each, with paid API and desktop app plans for higher volume. Squoosh is completely free and open source, with no limits on image count or file size, because all processing happens locally in your browser.</p>

    <h3>Does image compression reduce quality?</h3>
    <p>Both TinyPNG and Squoosh use lossy compression by default, which does remove some data, but the reduction is optimized to be virtually invisible to the human eye at normal viewing sizes. Squoosh also offers lossless options for users who need to preserve every pixel. For most web and marketing use, the quality loss is imperceptible.</p>

    <h3>Which format should I use: JPEG, PNG, WebP, or AVIF?</h3>
    <p>Use JPEG for photographs, PNG for images with transparency or text, WebP for modern web projects (about 30% smaller than JPEG), and AVIF for maximum compression (about 50% smaller than JPEG) if browser support is not a concern. Squoosh lets you compare all formats side by side, while TinyPNG focuses on PNG, JPEG, and WebP.</p>

    <h3>Can I compress multiple images at once with TinyPNG or Squoosh?</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG supports batch compression: you can drag up to 20 images onto the web interface at once, and the paid TinyPNG API and desktop apps handle unlimited batch processing. Squoosh processes one image at a time in the browser, which makes batch compression impractical for more than a few images.</p>

    <h3>Do TinyPNG and Squoosh upload my images to a server?</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG uploads your images to its servers for processing, then deletes them after a short period. Squoosh processes images entirely locally in your browser using WebAssembly, so your images never leave your device. For users with strict privacy or security requirements, Squoosh is the safer choice.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for WordPress or Shopify sites?</h3>
    <p>TinyPNG is better for WordPress and Shopify sites because it offers official plugins that automatically compress images on upload. The TinyPNG WordPress plugin and Shopify app handle compression in the background without any manual work. Squoosh has no CMS integrations, so you would need to compress images manually before uploading them to your CMS.</p>

